-- Q1: medium / L2 — Comprehensive income calculation (OCI items)
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Wellspring Technologies reports net income of $840,000 for 2025. During the year, the company also recorded a $55,000 unrealized gain on available-for-sale debt securities and a $30,000 foreign currency translation loss. Under SFAC No. 8, what is Wellspring''s total comprehensive income for 2025?', '["$755,000", "$865,000", "$810,000", "$925,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under SFAC No. 8 and ASC 220, comprehensive income includes net income plus other comprehensive income (OCI). Wellspring''s comprehensive income equals $840,000 + $55,000 unrealized gain − $30,000 translation loss = $865,000. Choice A ($755,000) incorrectly subtracts the unrealized gain, treating it as a reduction rather than an addition to net income.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Systematic allocation for expense recognition
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Ironside Manufacturing pays $720,000 on January 1, 2025, for a three-year equipment maintenance contract covering 2025 through 2027. The maintenance services are provided evenly throughout the contract period. Applying the FASB Conceptual Framework''s expense recognition principles, what amount should Ironside report as maintenance expense for 2025?', '["$720,000", "$360,000", "$240,000", "$180,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under SFAC No. 5 (ASC 235) and the systematic and rational allocation approach, costs consumed evenly over multiple periods should be allocated proportionally. The $720,000 three-year contract yields $240,000 per year ($720,000 ÷ 3). Choice A is incorrect because it expenses the full $720,000 when paid rather than allocating to the periods benefited.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Principles-based vs rules-based standards trade-off
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'During an accounting policy forum, one panelist argues that IFRS''s principles-based approach better reflects economic substance through management judgment, while another contends this reduces comparability and enables earnings manipulation. A third notes that U.S. GAAP''s prescriptive bright-line thresholds may encourage structuring transactions for desired outcomes. Evaluate which statement best describes the recognized trade-off.', '["Principles-based standards eliminate the need for professional judgment, making financial statements more comparable", "Rules-based standards prevent all forms of earnings management because they leave no room for interpretation", "Both approaches involve trade-offs, but rules-based standards risk encouraging form-over-substance structuring around bright-line thresholds", "The two approaches produce identical financial reporting outcomes when applied to the same transactions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per ASC 105 and the SEC''s 2003 principles-based study, both approaches involve trade-offs. Principles-based standards permit judgment that better reflects economic substance but may reduce comparability. Rules-based standards provide clear thresholds but risk encouraging structuring transactions around bright lines — prioritizing form over substance. Choice B is incorrect because rules-based standards do not eliminate earnings management; they redirect it toward transaction structuring.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Revenue element definition applied to scenario
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Cornerstone Retail LLC earns $125,000 from its primary merchandise sales during December 2025. Under the FASB Conceptual Framework, this inflow is classified as which element of financial statements?', '["Revenue from ongoing operations", "Gain from a peripheral transaction", "Total comprehensive income", "Owner-contributed capital"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under SFAC No. 8 (ASC 606), revenues are inflows or enhancements of assets from delivering goods or services that constitute the entity''s ongoing major or central operations. Merchandise sales represent Cornerstone''s primary business activity, making this revenue. Choice B is incorrect because gains apply only to peripheral or incidental transactions, such as selling unused equipment.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Exit price in fair value measurement (ASC 820)
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Harmon Industries holds a specialized machine with a carrying amount of $450,000. The company estimates it would cost $520,000 to replace the machine, and a willing buyer in an orderly transaction would pay $410,000 for it. Under ASC 820, what is the fair value of this machine?', '["$450,000", "$520,000", "$410,000", "$485,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 820-10-35-2, fair value is defined as the exit price — the price that would be received to sell an asset in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date. The $410,000 represents this exit price. Choice B ($520,000) is incorrect because replacement cost is an entry price, not the exit price that ASC 820 requires.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Articulation between financial statements
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Clearwater Corp. reports net income of $620,000, declares dividends of $150,000, and records a $40,000 unrealized loss on available-for-sale debt securities during 2025. Beginning retained earnings was $2,100,000. Analyze how the articulation principle connects these items to determine ending retained earnings.', '["$2,530,000", "$2,570,000", "$2,610,000", "$2,720,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 220 and the articulation principle, financial statements are interrelated. Retained earnings is affected only by net income and dividends, not OCI items. Ending retained earnings = $2,100,000 + $620,000 − $150,000 = $2,570,000. The $40,000 unrealized loss flows to accumulated OCI, not retained earnings. Choice A ($2,530,000) is incorrect because it deducts the OCI loss from retained earnings.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Fair value hierarchy levels (ASC 820)
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Meridian Capital holds three investments: (1) shares of a publicly traded stock quoted at $89 per share on the NYSE, (2) corporate bonds valued using observable yield curves of similar instruments, and (3) a private equity stake valued using a discounted cash flow model with significant unobservable assumptions. Under ASC 820, what are the fair value hierarchy levels for investments 1, 2, and 3, respectively?', '["Level 2, Level 1, Level 3", "Level 1, Level 1, Level 2", "Level 1, Level 3, Level 2", "Level 1, Level 2, Level 3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 820-10-35-37 through 35-54, Level 1 uses quoted prices in active markets (the NYSE stock). Level 2 uses observable inputs other than Level 1 quotes, such as comparable yield curves (the corporate bonds). Level 3 uses significant unobservable inputs like management''s DCF assumptions (the private equity). Choice A is incorrect because it reverses Levels 1 and 2, misclassifying the quoted stock as Level 2 rather than Level 1.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Derecognition of financial assets (ASC 860)
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Oakmont Leasing transfers a portfolio of receivables with a carrying amount of $1,200,000 to Granite Financial for $1,150,000 cash. Oakmont retains a subordinated interest that absorbs the first 10% of losses and continues to service the receivables. Under ASC 860, analyze whether Oakmont should derecognize the transferred receivables.', '["Oakmont should not derecognize because it retains significant risk through the subordinated interest and has not surrendered control", "Oakmont should derecognize and record a $50,000 loss on the transfer", "Oakmont should derecognize only the portion of receivables equal to the cash received", "Oakmont should record the transfer as a secured borrowing only if the receivables are past due"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 860-10-40-5, derecognition requires that the transferor surrenders control — transferred assets must be isolated, the transferee can pledge or exchange them, and the transferor does not maintain effective control. Oakmont''s subordinated interest absorbing first losses indicates it has not surrendered control. Choice B is incorrect because derecognition is not permitted when the transferor retains significant continuing involvement through loss subordination.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Financial capital maintenance concept
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Birchwood Holdings reports beginning net assets of $3,800,000 and ending net assets of $4,350,000 for 2025. During the year, owners invested $200,000 of additional capital and received $125,000 in distributions. Under the financial capital maintenance concept in SFAC No. 8, what is Birchwood''s comprehensive income for 2025?', '["$550,000", "$475,000", "$350,000", "$225,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 220 and SFAC No. 8 Chapter 4, financial capital maintenance defines comprehensive income as the change in net assets excluding owner transactions. Income = ($4,350,000 − $3,800,000) − $200,000 investments by owners + $125,000 distributions = $475,000. Choice A ($550,000) is incorrect because it includes owner transactions in income by using the raw change in net assets.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Losses element definition
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Which statement correctly distinguishes losses from expenses under the FASB Conceptual Framework?', '["Losses arise from peripheral or incidental transactions, while expenses arise from ongoing major operations", "Losses are recognized only when cash is paid, while expenses are recognized on the accrual basis", "Losses reduce liabilities, while expenses reduce assets", "Losses and expenses are identical elements reported in different sections of the income statement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under SFAC No. 8 (ASC 105), losses are decreases in equity from peripheral or incidental transactions, while expenses are outflows from delivering goods or services in the entity''s ongoing major operations. The key distinction is the nature of the activity — central vs. peripheral. Choice D is incorrect because losses and expenses are separate elements with different definitions, not merely different presentation locations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Distributions to owners element
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Under the FASB Conceptual Framework, what are distributions to owners?', '["Decreases in equity from transferring assets or incurring liabilities to owners", "Increases in equity from owner contributions of cash or other assets", "Decreases in net assets from the entity''s ongoing business operations", "Increases in liabilities resulting from obligations owed to shareholders"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under SFAC No. 8 (referenced in ASC 105), distributions to owners are decreases in equity resulting from transferring assets, rendering services, or incurring liabilities to owners. They are distinct from expenses, which arise from ongoing operations. Choice B is incorrect because it describes investments by owners, which increase rather than decrease equity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — Recognition vs disclosure evaluation (ASC 450)
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Pinnacle Aerospace discovers a $3,600,000 environmental remediation obligation. Management estimates a 40% probability of regulatory enforcement within five years and believes footnote disclosure is sufficient. The auditor argues a present obligation exists from past contamination regardless of enforcement probability. Under ASC 450-20, evaluate whether Pinnacle should recognize the liability or limit treatment to footnote disclosure.', '["Recognize the full $3,600,000 because any environmental obligation automatically meets the probable threshold", "Neither recognize nor disclose because the 40% probability falls below the reasonably possible threshold", "Recognize the liability because the Conceptual Framework''s definition of a liability does not require probability thresholds", "Disclose in the notes because ASC 450-20 requires recognition only when the loss is probable and reasonably estimable, and 40% does not meet probable"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 450-20-25-2, a loss contingency is recognized only when the loss is both probable (generally >75%) and reasonably estimable. At 40%, the loss is reasonably possible but not probable, requiring footnote disclosure under ASC 450-20-50-3. Choice C is incorrect because it confuses the Conceptual Framework''s broad liability definition with ASC 450''s specific recognition criteria, which impose a probability threshold the framework does not override.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Reporting entity concept boundaries
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Greenfield Holdings owns 100% of Alpha Corp. and 100% of Beta Corp. Alpha Corp. owns 80% of Gamma Inc. The CFO considers preparing separate financial statements for Alpha Corp. alone, excluding Beta Corp. and Gamma Inc. Under the reporting entity concept in SFAC No. 8, how should the reporting entity boundaries be determined?', '["Each legal entity must always prepare standalone financial statements regardless of ownership", "The reporting entity should encompass activities controlled by Greenfield, including Alpha, Beta, and Gamma, in consolidated statements", "Only entities with more than 90% ownership qualify for consolidation", "The reporting entity is limited to entities that share the same industry classification"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 810 and SFAC No. 8 Chapter 2, a reporting entity encompasses economic activity useful to investors, lenders, and creditors. When a parent controls subsidiaries, consolidated statements provide the most useful information about the group as one economic entity. Choice A is incorrect because the framework prioritizes economic substance over legal form — entities under common control are reported as one entity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Notes to financial statements purpose
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Under the FASB Conceptual Framework, what is the primary purpose of notes to financial statements?', '["To replace the information presented on the face of the financial statements", "To correct errors discovered after the financial statements are issued", "To provide additional information that supplements and explains amounts recognized in the statements", "To present management''s opinion on the entity''s future profitability"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under SFAC No. 8 and ASC 235-10-50, notes to financial statements provide information that supplements and explains items recognized on the face of the statements. Notes are integral to a complete set of financial statements, not a replacement. Choice A is incorrect because notes complement rather than replace face amounts — recognition and note disclosure serve different but complementary roles.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: hard / L3 — Measurement uncertainty effect on recognition
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Cascadia Biotech has developed a proprietary compound that preliminary studies suggest could have commercial value. Internal scientists estimate the compound''s value between $2,000,000 and $8,000,000, but regulatory approval is uncertain and no comparable compounds exist. The controller argues the asset should be recognized at $5,000,000. Under the FASB Conceptual Framework, analyze how measurement uncertainty affects this recognition decision.', '["The asset should not be recognized because high measurement uncertainty means the information would lack sufficient faithful representation to be useful", "The asset should be recognized at $2,000,000, reflecting the most conservative estimate available", "The asset should be recognized at $8,000,000 because the framework requires maximum transparency about potential value", "The asset should be recognized at $5,000,000 because the midpoint provides the most neutral measurement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under SFAC No. 8 Chapter 3 (ASC 105), recognition requires that an item meets an element definition and can be measured with sufficient faithful representation. When measurement uncertainty is extreme — a $2M to $8M range with unresolved regulatory outcomes — any point estimate may lack sufficient reliability to be useful. Choice D is incorrect because neutrality alone does not overcome the problem that the wide estimation range makes any point estimate potentially misleading.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — EITF role in standard-setting process
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Marshfield Corp.''s controller encounters a novel digital asset licensing transaction and finds no specific FASB ASC guidance. The controller discovers that the Emerging Issues Task Force recently reached a consensus on a similar issue. Under the U.S. GAAP standard-setting process, analyze the authoritative weight of this EITF consensus.', '["EITF consensuses are merely advisory opinions with no authoritative standing", "EITF consensuses are nonauthoritative, similar to textbooks and industry practice", "EITF consensuses are considered only when no other guidance exists in the ASC", "EITF consensuses ratified by the FASB are incorporated into the ASC and carry the same authoritative weight as any other codified guidance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 105-10-05-1, the FASB ASC is the sole source of authoritative U.S. GAAP for nongovernmental entities. EITF consensuses, once ratified by the FASB, are codified as Accounting Standards Updates and become part of the ASC with full authoritative status. Choice A is incorrect because EITF consensuses, after FASB ratification, are not advisory — they carry the same weight as standards originating directly from the FASB.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Investments by owners element
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Under the FASB Conceptual Framework, what are investments by owners?', '["Decreases in equity resulting from distributing assets to shareholders", "Increases in equity from transfers of resources to an entity by its owners", "Revenue earned from investing activities reported on the income statement", "Assets held by the entity for long-term capital appreciation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under SFAC No. 8 (ASC 105), investments by owners are increases in equity of a business enterprise resulting from transfers to it from other entities to obtain or increase ownership interests. They are equity transactions, not income statement items. Choice C is incorrect because it confuses the element with investment income — an income statement item unrelated to owner equity contributions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Cash basis vs accrual basis limitations
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Eastbridge Consulting LLC switched from cash basis to accrual basis in 2025. At year-end, the company had $175,000 of services performed but not yet billed, $92,000 of prepaid office rent covering January through March 2026, and $63,000 of accrued salaries payable. Analyze which items would not appear on a strict cash basis balance sheet.', '["Only the accrued salaries payable would be absent", "Only the unbilled services receivable would be absent", "The unbilled receivable, prepaid rent asset, and accrued salaries would all be absent under strict cash basis", "All three items would appear on both cash and accrual basis balance sheets"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 105-10-05 and the special purpose framework guidance, strict cash basis records transactions only when cash changes hands. The $175,000 unbilled receivable and $63,000 accrued liability do not appear because no cash was exchanged. The $92,000 prepaid rent is fully expensed when paid rather than deferred as an asset. Choice A is incorrect because cash basis omits all accrued items, not just payables.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Private Company Council alternatives eligibility
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Redmond Family Enterprises, a privately held company, adopted the PCC accounting alternative for goodwill under ASC 350-20-35-62, which allows amortization over 10 years and a simplified impairment test. The company is now considering an IPO. Analyze how going public would affect Redmond''s use of this alternative.', '["Redmond must discontinue the PCC alternative upon becoming a public business entity because these alternatives are restricted to entities that do not meet the public business entity definition", "Redmond may continue using the PCC alternative indefinitely because the election is irrevocable once adopted", "Redmond must retrospectively restate all prior financial statements as if it had never elected the alternative", "Redmond may continue using the alternative for a five-year transition period after the IPO"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 350-20-35-62 through 35-67, the PCC accounting alternatives are available only to entities that are not public business entities as defined in the ASC Master Glossary. When Redmond completes its IPO and meets the public business entity definition, it loses eligibility and must apply public company guidance. Choice B is incorrect because PCC elections are conditional on entity status, not permanent — a change in status requires adopting public entity guidance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — FASB due process steps
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Under FASB due process, what document does the FASB issue to solicit public comment before finalizing a new standard?', '["Staff Accounting Bulletin", "Accounting Standards Update", "Concepts Statement", "Exposure Draft"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 105 and the FASB''s due process procedures, the Board issues an Exposure Draft to solicit public comment on proposed standards before finalizing guidance. After considering comments, the FASB issues an Accounting Standards Update (ASU) to amend the Codification. Choice B is incorrect because an ASU is the final product of standard-setting, not the preliminary document used to gather public input.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Income approach valuation (ASC 820)
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Oakdale Energy holds a natural gas royalty interest that generates estimated annual cash flows of $180,000 for the next eight years. Using a discount rate of 10% that reflects the risk of the cash flows, the present value is $960,000. Under ASC 820, Oakdale applies the income approach to determine fair value. What fair value amount should Oakdale report?', '["$960,000", "$1,440,000", "$180,000", "$1,260,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 820-10-35-32, the income approach converts future amounts to a single present value using a discount rate reflecting the risk of those cash flows. The $960,000 represents fair value because it incorporates market expectations about timing, amount, and uncertainty. Choice B ($1,440,000) is incorrect because it uses the undiscounted sum rather than the present value, ignoring the time value of money central to the income approach.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Interim financial reporting treatment evaluation
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Lakeside Industries reports quarterly to its bank. In 2025, the company incurs $280,000 in annual employee training costs benefiting the full year and $90,000 in seasonal snow removal costs benefiting only Q1. The controller records $70,000 training expense per quarter and $90,000 snow removal entirely in Q1. A board member questions the different treatments. Under ASC 270, evaluate whether the controller''s approach is correct.', '["Both costs should be expensed entirely in the quarter incurred because interim periods are discrete reporting periods", "Both costs should be allocated evenly across all four quarters because interim periods are part of the annual period", "The controller is correct — annual-benefit training costs should be allocated evenly, while seasonal costs benefiting only Q1 should be recognized in Q1", "Neither cost should appear in interim statements because they are nonrecurring in nature"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 270-10-45-1 through 45-3, an interim period is viewed as an integral part of the annual period. Costs benefiting the entire year (annual training) should be allocated across quarters, while costs clearly associated with a specific period (seasonal snow removal in Q1) should be recognized in that period. Choice A incorrectly applies the discrete-period view, ignoring the integral relationship between interim and annual periods.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — FASB ASC organizational structure
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'What is the organizational structure of the FASB Accounting Standards Codification?', '["Volumes, chapters, articles, and clauses", "Topics, subtopics, sections, and paragraphs", "Titles, parts, subparts, and regulations", "Standards, interpretations, bulletins, and opinions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 105-10-05, the FASB ASC is organized into topics, subtopics, sections, and paragraphs. For example, ASC 606-10-25-1 refers to Topic 606, Subtopic 10, Section 25, Paragraph 1. Choice D is incorrect because it describes the pre-Codification structure of legacy pronouncements (SFAS, FIN, APB opinions), which were reorganized into the current structure effective July 1, 2009.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Conceptual framework vs specific ASC guidance override
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'A CPA preparing financial statements for Thornberry Industries argues that recognizing internally generated goodwill is consistent with the Conceptual Framework because it meets the asset definition — a controlled resource with expected future economic benefits. The controller counters that ASC 350-20-25-3 explicitly prohibits recognition. Evaluate which position should prevail and why.', '["The CPA is correct because the Conceptual Framework takes precedence over individual Codification topics", "The controller is correct, but only because internally generated goodwill fails to meet the asset definition", "The CPA is correct because any item meeting the Conceptual Framework''s asset definition must be recognized", "The controller is correct because specific ASC guidance overrides the Conceptual Framework, which is nonauthoritative and does not establish GAAP"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 105-10-05-1, the Codification is the sole source of authoritative GAAP. FASB Concepts Statements are explicitly nonauthoritative — they inform standard-setting but do not establish recognition requirements. ASC 350-20-25-3 prohibits capitalizing internally generated goodwill, and this specific prohibition controls regardless of the asset definition. Choice A is incorrect because the framework''s definitions are aspirational guidance for the Board, not binding rules for preparers.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Tax basis special purpose framework
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Sprucewood Contracting LLC prepares financial statements using the income tax basis of accounting. In December 2025, the company completes a $340,000 project but will not collect payment until February 2026. Sprucewood uses the cash method for tax purposes. Under the tax basis special purpose framework, what revenue amount does Sprucewood report for December 2025?', '["$340,000 recognized upon project completion", "$170,000 as partial revenue allocation", "$0 because cash has not been received", "$340,000 less estimated uncollectible amounts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 105 and the tax basis special purpose framework, revenue and expenses follow the entity''s income tax method. Since Sprucewood uses the cash method for tax purposes, revenue is recognized when cash is received, not when services are completed. The $340,000 is reported as 2026 revenue when collected. Choice A incorrectly applies accrual basis recognition to a tax basis framework.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Full disclosure principle
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Under the FASB Conceptual Framework, what does the full disclosure principle require?', '["Financial statements must include all information necessary for users to make informed economic decisions", "Entities must disclose proprietary business strategies to competitors", "Only transactions exceeding a specific dollar threshold must be reported", "Financial statements must predict future cash flows with certainty"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 235-10-50 and SFAC No. 8, the full disclosure principle requires financial statements to include all information significant enough to influence an informed user''s judgment, including notes, supplementary schedules, and parenthetical disclosures. Choice C is incorrect because disclosure is driven by qualitative materiality and user needs, not by arbitrary dollar thresholds alone.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Inline XBRL filing requirements for SEC registrants
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Sterling Pharmaceuticals, an SEC registrant, files its annual 10-K using Inline XBRL as required by SEC rules. A financial analyst asks whether the XBRL-tagged data carries the same legal responsibility as the traditional HTML filing. Analyze the regulatory status of Inline XBRL filings.', '["XBRL data is considered supplemental and carries no legal liability for the registrant", "Inline XBRL data is part of the official filing and subject to the same legal requirements, including officer certifications under SOX Section 302", "XBRL filings are reviewed only by automated systems and are exempt from SEC enforcement actions", "Inline XBRL is optional for all SEC registrants regardless of filer size"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under SEC Release No. 33-10514, Inline XBRL data embedded in the HTML filing is part of the official filing submitted to the SEC. It is subject to the same legal requirements as the underlying financial statements, including Sarbanes-Oxley Section 302 officer certifications. Choice A is incorrect because the SEC eliminated the supplemental treatment in 2018 when it mandated Inline XBRL, making tagged data integral to the filing.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — General-purpose vs special-purpose statements evaluation
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Birchmont Holdings operates medical clinics. Its primary lender requires monthly cash-basis financial statements for loan compliance, while minority investors request GAAP-basis statements to evaluate their equity investment. The controller proposes preparing only cash-basis statements for both parties. Under the FASB Conceptual Framework, evaluate whether this approach adequately serves both user groups.', '["Cash-basis statements are sufficient because all users can derive accrual information from cash flow data", "Cash-basis statements satisfy the lender but not the investors, since only special-purpose users contractually agree to accept a non-GAAP framework", "Both user groups are equally served because cash-basis and accrual-basis statements contain identical economic information", "The investors should accept cash-basis statements because the Conceptual Framework does not distinguish between accounting frameworks"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 105 and SSARS, cash-basis statements are a special purpose framework suitable for users who contractually agree to receive non-GAAP information, such as a lender under a loan covenant. General-purpose investors evaluating equity need GAAP-basis statements with accrual information about receivables, payables, and deferred items. Choice C is incorrect because cash-basis statements omit material accrual information that equity investors require for informed decision-making.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Consistency enhancing qualitative characteristic
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Under the FASB Conceptual Framework, which enhancing qualitative characteristic requires that an entity use the same accounting methods from period to period?', '["Timeliness", "Verifiability", "Understandability", "Consistency"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under SFAC No. 8 (ASC 250), consistency requires the same accounting methods be applied to similar transactions from period to period within the same entity. Consistency contributes to comparability but is distinct — comparability refers to cross-entity comparison, while consistency refers to uniformity over time. Choice A is incorrect because timeliness relates to information availability, not method uniformity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Stewardship objective in SFAC No. 8
(2, 'Conceptual Framework and Standards', 'Summit Resources Inc. posts record earnings of $12,400,000 in 2025, but a large institutional investor is more concerned about whether the CEO''s $8,000,000 capital expenditure was a prudent use of shareholder resources. The investor argues the income statement alone is insufficient. Under SFAC No. 8, analyze how the stewardship objective influences what financial reporting should provide.', '["SFAC No. 8 recognizes that financial reporting should provide information useful for assessing management''s stewardship of the entity''s resources, supporting the investor''s concern", "Stewardship is not addressed in SFAC No. 8 because the framework focuses exclusively on future cash flow prediction", "The stewardship objective requires auditors, not preparers, to evaluate the prudence of management''s decisions", "Stewardship information is limited to the statement of cash flows and is not relevant to other financial statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under SFAC No. 8, OB4 (ASC 105), the objective of general-purpose financial reporting includes providing information useful for assessing management''s stewardship of economic resources. Reports should help users evaluate how efficiently management has used entrusted resources. Choice B is incorrect because SFAC No. 8 explicitly addresses stewardship alongside — not subordinate to — cash flow prediction as dual objectives of financial reporting.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
